# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tozed P11IDU - LED defaults
#
# Insert into:  target/linux/ramips/mt76x8/base-files/etc/board.d/01_leds
# Indentation:  case arm at COLUMN 0, body indented ONE TAB (note: this
#               differs from 02_network, which is tab-indented throughout).
#
# STATUS: PROPOSAL. Unlike 02_network, this file has no tozed,p11idu arm on
# the running unit - grep -rn tozed /etc/board.d/01_leds comes back empty -
# so on hardware the LEDs are simply left on their kernel default (no
# trigger). Everything below is a starting point for submission, not
# something observed in service.
#
# The four GPIO LED names were verified against sysfs on the running unit:
#
#     $ ls /sys/class/leds/
#     green:wan   mt76-phy0   red:wan   red:wlan   voice
#
# which in turn confirms the DTS colour/function pairs - LED_COLOR_ID_RED
# is 1 and LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN is 2, so the lte_red/lte_green node names
# line up with their declared colours, and the wlan LED is red, not green.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---8<--- case arm: tozed,p11idu ---
tozed,p11idu)
	ucidef_set_led_switch "wan" "wan" "green:wan" "switch0" "0x14"
	ucidef_set_led_netdev "wifi" "wifi" "red:wlan" "phy0-ap0"
	;;
# ---8<--- /case arm ---

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What this does, and what it deliberately leaves alone
#
# green:wan  <- switch0 port mask 0x14, i.e. bit 2 | bit 4, the two ports
#               that docs/01 identifies as WAN 1 / WAN 2. Lights when the
#               wired WAN side has link.
#
# red:wlan   <- netdev trigger on phy0-ap0, the 2.4 GHz AP interface. The
#               name phy0-ap0 was confirmed on the running unit
#               (ls /sys/class/net/ -> br-lan eth0 eth0.1 lo phy0-ap0
#               phy0-sta0); there is no wlan0 on this board, so the
#               wlan0 spelling used by some older entries would never fire.
#
# red:wan    <- LEFT UNCONFIGURED ON PURPOSE. The DTS node is called
#               lte_red and its sibling lte_green, which strongly suggests
#               the pair are LTE/modem status lamps rather than wired-WAN
#               lamps. Wiring red:wan to a switch or netdev trigger could
#               therefore drive it wrongly. Needs a unit with a live modem.
#
# voice      <- LEFT UNCONFIGURED ON PURPOSE. GPIO 46 carries a bare
#               `label = "voice"` with no function/colour, i.e. the vendor
#               used the legacy LED API for it. No obvious trigger exists
#               for a VoIP indicator on a board with no telephony stack.
#
# If the lte_* reading turns out to be right, green:wan above should be
# dropped rather than retargeted - see the note in README.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
